  "evaluation": "A is best: it identifies the permitted basis for an apparent disagreement—a depression dimension within a quality-of-life measure versus a full depression scale—and also notes that Study B's score increase represents improvement. B ranks next because it recognizes the quality-of-life item, though its justification rests mainly on score direction rather than the difference in what was measured. C correctly spots the misleading harm label but relies entirely on score direction, which is not one of the rules' two specified grounds for an apparent verdict. All three correctly treat vitamin D-containing combination interventions as on-question.",
